With the discrete no-arbitrage logic established, the lecture returns to the famous continuous-time formula. The slide gives the call price as $C=SN(d_1)-e^{-rT}EN(d_2),$ with $\begin{aligned}
d_1&=\frac{\ln(S/E)+rT+\sigma^2T/2}{\sigma\sqrt{T}},\\
d_2&=\frac{\ln(S/E)+rT-\sigma^2T/2}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}.\end{aligned}$ It is useful to note the standard simplification $d_2=d_1-\sigma\sqrt{T}.$ Here $N(\cdot)$ is the cumulative normal distribution function, $r$ is the interest rate, and $\sigma$ is the standard deviation of the stock-price change.

